#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265817 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229139 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "68148c93-9139-5deb-8a53-a05d63ec5930" :knowledge-point/title "Using Linearity Properties of Linear Differential Operators"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "09b0bb28-b0fb-5e8b-b3b7-46bc10b2c6b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+6x-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263090} {:answer/id #uuid "31948791-dcc1-59bd-a512-a8b1324e7e66" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263426} {:answer/id #uuid "375c88a1-f8d6-5547-a45f-b2cacbf90b4e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+12x-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263496} {:answer/id #uuid "6e577225-120c-5926-a558-ff3fdf544279" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263956} {:answer/id #uuid "ef96f515-25e7-5799-a911-9ff530d6058d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}-9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265102}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6e577225-120c-5926-a558-ff3fdf544279" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263956} :answer-field/id #uuid "fd9965ee-4811-54a2-aa77-27dcd857b9aa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265696}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "90434f36-e6cb-59b9-8b48-0ed0424bb2ec" :question/math-academy-id "q-130081" :question/problem "Suppose that $L$ is a linear differential operator such that $L({y}_{1})=3x-5$ and $L({y}_{2})=(x-3)^{2}$ for some functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x).$ What is $L(2{y}_{1}+{y}_{2})?$" :question/worked-solution "For any linear differential operator,\n\n$$\nL(a{y}_{1}(x)+b{y}_{2}(x))=aL({y}_{1}(x))+bL({y}_{2}(x))\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants. \n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(2{y}_{1}+{y}_{2}) & =2L({y}_{1})+L({y}_{2}) \\\\  & =2\\cdot (3x-5)+(x-3)^{2} \\\\  & =6x-10+{x}^{2}-6x+9 \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+(6x-6x)+(-10+9) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311885 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229138 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f5976004-053f-58ec-9d75-54d43a419397" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Linear Differential Operators Using the General Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d87b94e-2a58-5a91-b413-e2ace8c0acef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308839} {:answer/id #uuid "3977be65-6745-5b00-9e8b-2471e205c872" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309184} {:answer/id #uuid "62e91591-c9db-593a-9041-dbbc0a50e178" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309500} {:answer/id #uuid "7f143cd7-e11a-59b4-bd54-f611d460cc2d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309741} {:answer/id #uuid "d5ed839d-2200-51a0-8970-9289668f781e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310471}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "62e91591-c9db-593a-9041-dbbc0a50e178" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309500} :answer-field/id #uuid "0af460f7-9e3d-5968-8b8f-b6d594daad31" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310825}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7a6ed137-ea67-591f-bf1a-a0d1b0037ea7" :question/math-academy-id "q-337778" :question/problem "Which of the following are linear differential operators?\n\n- ${D}_{1}(y)=\\frac{1}{x+1}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\,y$\n- ${D}_{2}(y)=\\sqrt{x}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-2y$\n- ${D}_{3}(y)=2\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{3}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{3}}+{e}^{x}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}}\\,y$" :question/worked-solution "An $n$th-order differential operator $L$ is linear if it is of the following form:\n\n$$\nL(y)={f}_{n}(x)\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{n}}+{f}_{n-1}(x)\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n-1}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{n-1}}+⋯+{f}_{1}(x)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{f}_{0}(x)y\n$$\n\nwhere each ${f}_{i}$ is a function of $x$ only. \n\nWith that in mind, let's check each operator.\n\n- Operator ${D}_{1}$ is linear. It is a first-order linear differential operator of the form\n\n$$\n{D}_{1}(y)={f}_{1}(x)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{f}_{0}(x)y\n$$\n\nwith ${f}_{1}(x)=\\frac{1}{x+1}$ and ${f}_{0}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x).$\n\n- Operator ${D}_{2}$ is linear. It is a second-order linear differential operator of the form\n\n$$\n{D}_{2}(y)={f}_{2}(x)\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+{f}_{1}(x)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{f}_{0}(x)y\n$$\n\nwith ${f}_{2}(x)=\\sqrt{x},{f}_{1}(x)=-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x),$ and ${f}_{0}(x)=-2.$\n\n- Operator ${D}_{3}$ is linear. It is a third-order linear differential operator of the form\n\n$$\n{D}_{3}(y)={f}_{3}(x)\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{3}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{3}}+{f}_{2}(x)\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+{f}_{1}(x)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{f}_{0}(x)y\n$$\n\nwith ${f}_{3}(x)=2,{f}_{2}(x)={e}^{x},{f}_{1}(x)=0,$ and ${f}_{0}(x)=-\\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}]}
